        <div id="ah-vs-wh" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Amp-Hours, Watt-Hours and Usable Capacity
          </h2>

          <p>
            Batteries are sold in amp-hours, but your loads are measured in watts, so the number that matters for planning is watt-hours. For a 12V LiFePO4 battery, multiply amp-hours by 12.8 volts. A 100Ah battery is about 1,280Wh, a 200Ah battery about 2,560Wh, and so on. That is the total energy stored; what you can actually use depends on battery chemistry and how you treat it.
          </p>

          <p>
            This is where lithium pulls ahead. Most lead-acid deep-cycle batteries should not be discharged much below 50 percent if you want them to last, so a 100Ah lead-acid battery gives you roughly 50Ah, or about 600Wh, of usable energy. A 100Ah LiFePO4 battery can be used down to nearly empty without damage, so you get close to the full 1,280Wh. In practice, one lithium battery replaces two lead-acid batteries, and it weighs less than one of them.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE CONVERSION</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Watt-hours = amp-hours x 12.8 for a 12V LiFePO4 battery.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Then compare usable energy, not the label. 100Ah of lithium gives you roughly 1,200-1,280Wh you can use. 100Ah of lead-acid gives you about 600Wh if you stop at 50 percent. That is why a lithium swap usually doubles real capacity even at the same amp-hour rating.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Voltage behavior matters too. A lead-acid battery's voltage sags steadily as it discharges and drops further under heavy load, which makes inverters shut off on low voltage and makes lights dim and fans slow. LiFePO4 holds a nearly flat voltage, around 13V, for most of its discharge. That is great for appliances, but it also means you cannot read state of charge from voltage. Install a shunt-based battery monitor that counts amp-hours in and out; the voltmeter in an RV's monitor panel is close to useless with lithium.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, check how a battery's capacity is rated. Reputable LiFePO4 makers rate capacity at a moderate discharge current, and the numbers in this guide are the manufacturers' figures. Extremely cheap no-name batteries sometimes overstate capacity, which is one reason we stick to established brands even in the budget slot.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="bms" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What the BMS Does and Where It Limits You
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every LiFePO4 battery on this list has a battery management system inside. The BMS watches individual cell voltages, balances the cells so they stay matched, cuts off charging if voltage climbs too high, cuts off discharging before the cells are damaged by going too low, and disconnects the battery in a short circuit or overcurrent. Most also monitor temperature and block charging when the cells are too cold. It is the reason a drop-in lithium battery is safe to use without a separate controller.
          </p>

          <p>
            The BMS also sets a ceiling on how much current the battery can deliver. Many 100Ah batteries use a 100A continuous BMS, sometimes with a brief higher surge. At 12V, 100A is roughly 1,200W of DC, which works out to about 1,000W of AC through an inverter once losses are counted. Connect a 2,000W inverter to one such battery and run a microwave, and the BMS will shut the battery off to protect itself. That is the most common reason a new lithium setup "randomly" cuts out.
          </p>

          <p>
            The fix is to add batteries in parallel. Two 100Ah batteries with 100A BMS each can share a 200A load; three can share 300A. Use identical batteries of the same model and age, bring each to full charge before connecting them, and wire them with equal-length cables to a pair of busbars so current is shared evenly. A <a href="/best-power-inverter/">power inverter</a> sized beyond your bank's combined BMS rating will keep tripping, no matter how much capacity the batteries have.
          </p>

          <p>
            Series wiring for 24V or 48V is a different matter. Some 12V batteries permit series connections up to a stated limit and some do not, and the BMS in each battery cannot see the others, so batteries can drift out of balance over time. If you are building a large 24V or 48V system, a battery designed for that voltage is usually the better choice. Follow the manufacturer's instructions on series and parallel limits exactly.
          </p>

          <p>
            A few BMS designs also include Bluetooth monitoring, which shows state of charge, cell voltages and temperature on your phone. It is useful for diagnosing problems, especially low-temperature cutoffs. It does not replace a proper shunt battery monitor for the whole bank, but it is a nice extra when it is offered.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cold" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Cold Weather: Low-Temp Cutoff and Self-Heating
          </h2>

          <p>
            The one hard rule of LiFePO4 is that it must not be charged below freezing. Charging lithium cells below about 32°F (0°C) can plate metallic lithium onto the anode, which permanently reduces capacity and can damage the cell. Discharging in the cold is fine within limits; many makers rate their batteries to discharge down to around -4°F (-20°C). The asymmetry catches people out: the furnace fan runs all night on the battery, but the solar controller cannot recharge it the next morning.
          </p>

          <p>
            Good batteries handle this with a low-temperature charge cutoff in the BMS. When the cells are too cold, the battery simply refuses to accept a charge, protecting itself. That is the minimum you should accept. Cheap batteries without low-temp protection rely on you to remember, and a single frosty morning with solar charging can do lasting damage.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE COLD RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Never charge LiFePO4 below about 32°F unless the battery heats itself first.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Low-temperature cutoff protects the battery but leaves you unable to recharge it. Self-heating batteries use incoming charge power to warm the cells, then start charging. If you camp below freezing, buy one or keep the battery inside the heated envelope of the RV.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Self-heating batteries go further. When the cells are cold and a charging source is connected, they divert some of that incoming power into internal heating pads, then begin charging once the cells reach a safe temperature. The Renogy self-heating battery on this list works that way, and Battle Born, LiTime and SOK all sell heated versions. Heating uses energy, so on a cold morning the first stretch of solar production warms the battery before it stores anything.
          </p>

          <p>
            Location helps as much as hardware. A battery in an exterior tongue box on a trailer sees the outside temperature; the same battery under a dinette or in a heated basement bay may never get near freezing. If you are moving from lead-acid, consider relocating the batteries inside the living space. LiFePO4 does not vent gas during normal charging the way flooded lead-acid does, which makes indoor mounting practical, though the battery should still be secured and properly fused.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="charging" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Converter, Solar and Alternator Charging
          </h2>

          <p>
            A lithium battery is only as good as the charger feeding it, and most RVs built before lithium became common have converters set up for lead-acid. Many older converters charge at a lower voltage than lithium wants, so the battery never fills completely. Some apply equalization or desulfation modes that lithium should never see. Most 12V LiFePO4 makers specify charging at around 14.2-14.6V with little or no float. Check your battery's chart and set every charger to match.
          </p>

          <p>
            For shore and generator power, the cleanest fix is a lithium-capable converter. Makers such as Progressive Dynamics offer converters with a lithium charging mode that drop into the same space as the old unit. Solar charge controllers are usually easier: most MPPT controllers have a lithium or user-defined profile that you select during setup. Disable temperature compensation on any lead-acid-oriented controller, since LiFePO4 does not need it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Alternator charging is the part people get wrong most often. Lithium batteries accept current eagerly, and a deeply discharged lithium bank connected directly to a vehicle alternator can pull more current than the alternator is designed to deliver continuously, overheating it. A DC-DC charger solves this. It sits between the starting battery and the house bank, limits charging current to a safe level, applies the correct lithium profile and isolates the two batteries so the engine always starts.
          </p>

          <p>
            For tow vehicles charging a travel trailer through the 7-pin connector, the wiring is usually too thin to deliver much current, and voltage drop over that long run means the trailer battery barely charges while driving. A DC-DC charger mounted in the trailer, near the battery, makes the most of whatever arrives. For motorhomes and vans, a 30-50A DC-DC charger is a common and effective upgrade.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are building a system from scratch, choose the inverter, converter, solar controller and DC-DC charger together and check each one's lithium settings. Many RV owners pair the battery with a <a href="/best-portable-solar-panel/">portable solar panel</a> for boondocking; just make sure the controller is set to lithium before the first charge.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="swap" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Swapping Out Lead-Acid: A Checklist
          </h2>

          <p>
            Physically, most drop-in LiFePO4 batteries fit in the space a Group 24, 27 or 31 lead-acid battery occupies. Electrically, the swap involves more than moving cables. Start with the charging sources: converter, solar controller, DC-DC or alternator, and any inverter charger. Each needs a lithium profile or replacement. Skipping this step is the most common reason a lithium upgrade disappoints.
          </p>

          <p>
            Next, replace the old voltmeter-style battery indicator with a shunt-based monitor. Because LiFePO4 voltage stays nearly flat, the old indicator will read full until the battery is almost empty. A shunt measures every amp in and out and gives you a real percentage, which is the only way to manage a lithium bank well.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not mix lithium and lead-acid in the same bank. They charge to different voltages and behave differently under load, so the lead-acid battery will be overcharged or the lithium battery undercharged, and the stronger battery will try to charge the weaker one constantly. Replace the whole bank at once. Also confirm that the battery is used for house loads only. Most deep-cycle LiFePO4 batteries are not designed for engine starting, and some motorhome generators start from the house bank, so check your battery's cranking guidance before relying on it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check the fusing and cables. If the lithium upgrade comes with a bigger inverter, the original cables and fuse may be too small. Size the cable to the inverter's current and fit a fuse rated for that cable as close to the battery as practical; a Class T fuse is often recommended with lithium because of its high interrupt rating. Finally, look for parasitic draws. Propane detectors, radios and control boards draw small currents constantly. Use the battery disconnect switch in storage, or keep the battery on a maintenance charge with the correct lithium profile.
          </p>
        </div>

