          <p>
            A weekend camper with lights, a water pump, phone charging and a fan might use 300-600Wh a day, which one 100Ah lithium battery handles easily. Add a 12V fridge and a furnace on cold nights and you are at 1,000-2,000Wh, which suggests 200Ah. Full-time boondockers who run a residential fridge, a microwave and laptops often need 400Ah or more, plus solar to refill it.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE SIZING RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Daily watt-hours x days between charges x 1.2, then divide by 12.8.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">That gives you the amp-hours of LiFePO4 to buy. A camper using 1,200Wh a day who wants two days without charging needs about 1,200 x 2 x 1.2 / 12.8, or roughly 225Ah: two 100Ah batteries or one 206Ah battery.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Then check current. A bank's continuous output is the sum of its batteries' BMS ratings, and it must cover your inverter at full load. A 2,000W inverter needs roughly 200A of supply, so two 100A-rated batteries in parallel is the realistic minimum. If you plan to run induction cooktops or air conditioning from batteries, the current requirement rises sharply, and you are into the territory of a large bank, a powerful inverter charger and serious solar.
          </p>

          <p>
            Plan the charging side alongside the bank. A battery you can empty in a day but only refill over three days of solar is not much use. As a rough guide, the solar array and DC-DC charger together should replace most of a typical day's use on a typical day, with shore or generator power as the backup. A quiet <a href="/best-rv-generator/">RV generator</a> remains useful for cloudy stretches and air conditioning.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="portable" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Portable Lithium Battery Packs and Power Stations
          </h2>

          <p>
            Not everyone searching for a lithium battery needs a 12V deep-cycle unit bolted into a battery box. If you have a tent, a car camper, a truck without a house electrical system, or you want something that also comes into the house during a blackout, a self-contained rechargeable lithium battery may be the better buy. The most capable of those are portable power stations, though for running a whole house through a long outage a <a href="/best-home-generator-for-power-outages/">home generator</a> is still the bigger tool.
          </p>

          <p>
            A portable power station combines a lithium battery, often LiFePO4, with an inverter, a charger, a solar controller and a battery management system in one box with AC, USB and 12V outlets on the front. You charge it from the wall, the car or solar, and there is nothing to wire. Our <a href="/best-portable-power-station-for-camping/">camping power station picks</a> cover sizes from small weekend units to 2kWh models that rival a 200Ah RV battery.
          </p>

          <p>
            The trade-off is integration and cost per watt-hour. A power station cannot easily feed the RV's built-in 12V circuits, lights and water pump, and large models cost more per watt-hour than a bare LiFePO4 battery. For an RV or van with an existing electrical system, a lithium house battery is usually better value. For a trailer you do not want to rewire, a power station is a reasonable stopgap.
          </p>

          <p>
            At the small end, a portable lithium battery pack for phones and laptops is a different product altogether. For those, see our guides to the <a href="/best-laptop-power-bank/">best laptop power banks</a> and <a href="/best-solar-power-bank/">solar power banks</a>. They are lithium-ion rather than LiFePO4 in most cases and are measured in tens of watt-hours, not thousands.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="which" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Which Lithium RV Battery to Buy
          </h2>

          <p>
            If you want the safest bet on support and longevity, the Battle Born 100Ah is the best lithium RV battery for most owners who are willing to pay for it. One battery replaces two lead-acid batteries for typical weekend use; two give you a bank that supports a 2,000W inverter. If you camp in freezing weather, buy the heated version or the Renogy self-heating battery.
          </p>

          <p>
            If budget is the constraint, the LiTime 12V 100Ah gives you the same energy for far less, which often means you can afford 200Ah instead of 100Ah. Make sure you pick the variant with the cold-weather protection you need. For a single battery around 200Ah, the SOK 206Ah is a strong value, as long as it fits your compartment.
          </p>

          <p>
            Full-time RVers and boondockers building a big bank should look at the Battle Born 270Ah GC3, which simplifies wiring by replacing several small batteries. For boats, especially those running trolling motors alongside house loads, the Dakota Lithium 100Ah is a well-regarded alternative.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whatever you buy, budget for the supporting parts: a lithium-capable converter or charger, a DC-DC charger for alternator charging, a shunt battery monitor and the right fuse. Those parts are what make a lithium RV battery deliver on its promise. If your camping is more about occasional campsite power than a full RV system, a portable power station is the simpler choice, and our <a href="/ecoflow-vs-bluetti/">EcoFlow vs Bluetti comparison</a> is a good place to start.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Keeping the old lead-acid converter settings</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Many older RV converters charge too low for lithium or apply equalize and desulfation modes that lithium should never see. The result is a battery that never fills, or one that is stressed by the wrong profile. Set every charger to the battery maker's lithium voltages, or replace the converter with a lithium-capable model.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Charging below freezing</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                LiFePO4 must not be charged below about 32°F. Batteries with low-temperature cutoff refuse the charge, which protects them but leaves you without power; batteries without it can be permanently damaged. If you camp in the cold, buy a self-heating battery or mount the bank inside the heated living space.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running a big inverter on one battery</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A typical 100Ah battery has a 100A BMS, which is about 1,000W of AC output. Ask it for a 2,000W microwave load and it disconnects. Add batteries in parallel until the combined BMS rating covers the inverter at full load, and wire them with equal-length cables to busbars so current is shared evenly.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Charging lithium straight from the alternator</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A depleted lithium bank can pull more current than an alternator is designed to deliver continuously, and the alternator overheats. A DC-DC charger limits the current, applies the correct lithium profile and isolates the starting battery so the engine still cranks. It is a modest addition that protects a much more expensive part.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Mixing lithium with lead-acid</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Lithium and lead-acid charge to different voltages and sag differently under load, so a mixed bank constantly fights itself and neither battery lives long. Replace the whole bank at once, use identical batteries of the same model and age in parallel, and bring each to full charge before connecting them together.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

