        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best lithium RV battery for most people?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most RV owners, a 12V 100Ah LiFePO4 battery from an established brand is the right starting point. The Battle Born 100Ah is the premium choice with the strongest support; the LiTime 12V 100Ah offers the same rated energy for far less. Buy two if you run a 2,000W inverter or a fridge and furnace through cold nights.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many lithium batteries do I need for my RV?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Add up your daily watt-hours, multiply by the number of days between charges and by 1.2 for margin, then divide by 12.8 to get amp-hours. Weekend campers often need 100Ah. A fridge, furnace and laptops push most people to 200Ah, and full-time boondockers commonly run 400Ah or more with solar to recharge it.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I just drop a lithium battery into my RV?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Physically, usually yes; electrically, check the chargers first. Your converter, solar controller and any alternator connection need lithium-compatible settings. Replace the voltage-based battery gauge with a shunt monitor, confirm the cable and fuse sizes, and do not mix lithium with leftover lead-acid batteries. With those done, the swap is straightforward.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a LiFePO4 RV battery safe?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                LiFePO4 is the most thermally stable of the common lithium chemistries and does not vent gas during normal charging like flooded lead-acid. The built-in BMS protects against overcharge, over-discharge, short circuits and charging in the cold. As with any battery, fuse the positive cable close to the battery, secure the battery against movement and use properly crimped cables.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a DC-DC charger for a lithium RV battery?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                If you charge from a vehicle alternator, yes in most cases. Lithium accepts current so readily that a deeply discharged bank can overheat an alternator, and a DC-DC charger limits current, applies the correct lithium profile and keeps the starting battery isolated. For travel trailers charged through the 7-pin connector, it also makes the most of thin wiring.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can lithium RV batteries be used in the winter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, with care. LiFePO4 can usually discharge down to around -4°F, but it must not be charged below about 32°F. Batteries with low-temperature cutoff block charging until they warm up; self-heating batteries warm themselves using incoming charge power. Mounting the batteries inside the heated part of the RV is the simplest way to avoid the issue.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a portable power station better than a lithium RV battery?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on the setup. For an RV or van with built-in 12V circuits, a lithium house battery is cheaper per watt-hour and feeds the existing lights, pump and fans. For tents, car camping or a trailer you do not want to rewire, a portable power station with a LiFePO4 battery is simpler and can come into the house during an outage.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
